			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff/113358416621619317",
			"author": {
				"name": "jake lazaroff",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@jakelazaroff",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/829/533/556/740/279/original/69a92e79689324bc.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-10-23T19:36:07.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://ruby.social/@konnorrogers\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>konnorrogers</span></a></span> i think bluesky uses react-native-web to share their UI code between mobile platforms and the web, but none of the examples that i can find on <a href=\"https://necolas.github.io/react-native-web/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">necolas.github.io/react-native</span><span class=\"invisible\">-web/</span></a> generate inline styles so i'd guess they're doing something weird. definitely a dark corner of the JS framework world!</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113358416600626426",
			"parentId": "113358409813897202",
